<template id="guide-src">

<section class="part" id="part-one">
<p class="part-kicker">Part One</p>
<h1 class="part-title">What the Research Shows</h1>
<p class="lede">This half of the guide explains how fat loss works, using controlled feeding studies, clinical trials, and systematic reviews. Every factual claim carries a bracketed number that links to the full citation in the reference list at the end of the document. Where a finding comes from a small study or a narrow population, that is stated alongside it.</p>
</section>
<section id="s1">
<h2><span class="n">1</span>The Equation Is Right. The Inputs Are Not Simple.</h2>
<p>The suspicion that weight loss is more complicated than "eat less" is correct, but not because the energy balance equation is wrong. Energy is conserved. When you take in less energy than you use over a period of time, the difference comes out of storage, mostly body fat, and body weight falls <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-1">1</a>]</sup>. What makes the equation hard to use is that none of its terms is a fixed number you can look up, and several of them change in response to each other.</p>
<div class="eq"><div class="eq-line"><i>ΔE</i><sub>stored</sub> = <i>E</i><sub>absorbed</sub> − <i>E</i><sub>expended</sub></div><div class="eq-note">Change in stored energy equals energy absorbed from food minus energy expended by the body. Energy absorbed is not the same as the number on the label (Section 4), and energy expended shifts when you diet or train (Sections 2 and 3).</div></div>
<p>A 2024 review in the <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> pulled together the evidence on why people of the same size, eating the same amount, lose weight at different rates. Genetic variation, hormonal signaling, sleep, stress, and the type of exercise performed all change how much of a deficit is drawn from fat rather than muscle, and how strongly the body resists the deficit <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-2">2</a>]</sup>.</p>
<h3>The 3,500-calorie rule overpromises</h3>
<p>The familiar rule that 3,500 calories equals one pound of fat is a reasonable conversion for a single pound. Applied over months, it overestimates results, because a smaller body burns less energy. Modeling work published in <i>The Lancet</i> estimated that a permanent cut of 10 calories per day eventually lowers body weight by about 1 pound, with half of that change arriving in the first year and nearly all of it within about three years <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-3">3</a>]</sup>. By that estimate, cutting 100 calories a day for good produces roughly 10 pounds of eventual loss, not 10 pounds every year as the simple rule implies. This is why any fat-loss plan has to be adjusted as it goes.</p>
</section>
<section id="s2">
<h2><span class="n">2</span>Where Your Calories Actually Go</h2>
<p>Total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) is the sum of everything your body burns in 24 hours. It has four parts, and they are not equally large or equally changeable.</p>
<div class="eq"><div class="eq-line"><i>TDEE</i> = <i>BMR</i> + <i>TEF</i> + <i>EAT</i> + <i>NEAT</i></div></div>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:22%"/><col style="width:40%"/><col style="width:28%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Part</th><th class="">Full name</th><th class="">What it covers</th><th class="">Typical share</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class=""><b>BMR</b></td><td class="">Basal metabolic rate</td><td class="">Energy used at complete rest: breathing, circulation, brain activity, cell repair.</td><td class="">About 60% in sedentary adults <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-1">1</a>]</sup></td></tr><tr class=""><td class=""><b>TEF</b></td><td class="">Thermic effect of food</td><td class="">Energy used to digest, absorb, and store food.</td><td class="">About 10% on a mixed diet <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-4">4</a>]</sup></td></tr><tr class=""><td class=""><b>EAT</b></td><td class="">Exercise activity thermogenesis</td><td class="">Planned exercise, such as a CrossFit class.</td><td class="">Varies with training</td></tr><tr class=""><td class=""><b>NEAT</b></td><td class="">Non-exercise activity thermogenesis</td><td class="">All other movement: walking, standing, commuting, climbing stairs, fidgeting, chores.</td><td class="">From about 6-10% in very sedentary people to 50% or more in very active people <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-5">5</a>]</sup></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>NEAT: the part nobody counts</h3>
<p>In a classic experiment at the Mayo Clinic, 16 lean adults were overfed by 1,000 calories a day for eight weeks. Fat gain varied roughly tenfold between people, from under half a kilogram to over four kilograms. The strongest predictor of who stayed lean was not exercise or metabolic rate. It was the change in NEAT, which ranged from a drop of 98 calories a day to an increase of 692 calories a day as some people simply started moving more without being told to <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-6">6</a>]</sup>.</p>
<p>A follow-up study fitted people with sensors that recorded posture and movement every half second for ten days. Participants with obesity sat about 164 minutes a day longer than lean participants. The researchers estimated that adopting the lean group's movement pattern would burn an extra 350 calories a day <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-7">7</a>]</sup>.</p>
<p>The type of movement matters. A meta-analysis found that standing burns only about 0.15 calories per minute more than sitting, which is roughly 9 calories per hour <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-8">8</a>]</sup>. Walking is a different story: even a slow 1 mile per hour stroll roughly doubles energy use compared with sitting, about 100 extra calories per hour for a 70 kg (154 lb) person <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-9">9</a>]</sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><b>What the evidence supports:</b> NEAT is the most variable part of daily expenditure and the one most easily changed outside the gym. Walking moves it meaningfully. Standing alone does not.</p></div>
</section>
<section id="s3">
<h2><span class="n">3</span>Your Body Pushes Back</h2>
<p>When a calorie deficit is sustained, the body responds in ways that shrink the deficit. Researchers call this adaptive thermogenesis: a drop in energy expenditure larger than the change in body size explains. A clinical review notes that it can appear after as little as a 5% loss of body weight, and that by 10% it shows up in both resting and exercise energy expenditure <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-10">10</a>]</sup>.</p>
<h3>How large the effect is</h3>
<p>Rosenbaum and Leibel, who have studied this for decades, found that people holding a body weight 10% or more below their starting point burn 20 to 25% fewer calories per day, and that this is 10 to 15% below what the loss of fat and lean tissue alone would predict <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-11">11</a>]</sup>. Much of that extra drop shows up in the energy cost of movement, as muscles perform the same work more efficiently.</p>
<h3>Hunger rises</h3>
<p>In a study of 50 adults who lost weight on a ten-week, very-low-calorie diet, levels of leptin (a hormone that signals fullness) were still lower, levels of ghrelin (a hormone that signals hunger) were still higher, and self-reported hunger was still elevated one year later <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-12">12</a>]</sup>. Feeling hungrier as a diet goes on is a measurable biological response, not a lack of discipline.</p>
<h3>The extreme case</h3>
<p>Researchers followed 14 contestants from the television show "The Biggest Loser" for six years. Their resting metabolic rates remained about 500 calories per day lower than expected for their body size <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-13">13</a>]</sup>. These contestants lost an average of about 58 kg in 30 weeks, far beyond ordinary dieting, so the size of this effect should not be read as typical for modest fat loss.</p>
<h3>Exercise calories are partly offset</h3>
<p>A 2021 analysis of 1,754 adults whose daily energy use was measured with doubly labeled water found that, on average, 28% of the calories burned through extra activity were offset by a drop in basal energy expenditure. Only about 72% of the calories burned in activity showed up as extra calories burned that day. The offset was larger in people carrying more body fat <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-14">14</a>]</sup>.</p>
<h3>What seems to help</h3>
<p>In the MATADOR trial, men with obesity completed 16 weeks of dieting either continuously or in two-week blocks separated by two-week breaks at maintenance calories. The intermittent group lost more weight (about 14 kg compared with 9 kg) and more fat, showed less of a drop in resting metabolism, and kept more of the weight off six months later <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-15">15</a>]</sup>. The evidence for shorter breaks is thinner. In a seven-week trial of resistance-trained adults, two high-carbohydrate maintenance days each week produced the same fat loss as continuous dieting and better retention of dry lean mass; the authors also reported better preservation of resting metabolism, but a published reanalysis found that only the lean-mass difference was statistically reliable <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-16">16</a>]</sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><b>What the evidence supports:</b> A fixed calorie target loses effectiveness as the body adapts. Plans that include periods at maintenance and that are adjusted from real weight data hold up better than plans that assume expenditure stays constant.</p></div>
</section>
<section id="s4">
<h2><span class="n">4</span>The Label Is an Estimate</h2>
<p>Food labels in the United States are built on conversion factors from the late 1800s: roughly 4 calories per gram of protein and carbohydrate and 9 per gram of fat. These are averages. They do not account for how much of a specific food your gut actually absorbs.</p>
<h3>Almonds</h3>
<p>When USDA researchers measured what people actually absorbed from almonds in a controlled feeding study, a 28 g serving delivered about 129 calories, 32% fewer than the label value calculated from standard factors <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-17">17</a>]</sup>. Intact cell walls in whole nuts trap some of the fat, which passes through undigested.</p>
<h3>Whole grains</h3>
<p>In a six-week trial in which researchers provided all food to 81 adults, swapping refined grains for whole grains raised resting metabolic rate by about 43 calories per day and increased the calories lost in stool by about 57 per day. Combined, the whole-grain group came out 92 calories per day ahead, with the same calories on the plate <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-18">18</a>]</sup>.</p>
<h3>The gut microbiome</h3>
<p>A 2023 randomized trial in <i>Nature Communications</i> fed adults two diets with identical calories while they lived in sealed metabolic chambers that measure energy use precisely. One diet was high in fiber and resistant starch and used larger, less processed food particles, such as whole nuts instead of nut butter. The other was a typical processed Western diet. On the high-fiber diet, participants excreted an average of 116 more calories per day in stool, and absorption varied from person to person depending on their gut bacteria <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-19">19</a>]</sup>. A 2025 study from Arizona State University found that people whose gut microbes produce more methane extract more calories from the same fiber-rich meals <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-20">20</a>]</sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><b>What the evidence supports:</b> Minimally processed, high-fiber foods deliver somewhat fewer usable calories than their labels suggest, on the order of 50 to 120 calories per day across a whole diet in these trials. That does not override energy balance, but it tilts it in your favor.</p></div>
</section>
<section id="s5">
<h2><span class="n">5</span>Quality, Volume, and Fullness</h2>
<p>The idea that the volume and kind of food matter is well supported. Most of the effect works through appetite: how much people end up eating when they eat until satisfied.</p>
<h3>Ultra-processed food makes people eat more</h3>
<p>At the National Institutes of Health, 20 adults lived on a research ward for four weeks. For two weeks they were offered ultra-processed meals; for two weeks, unprocessed meals. The menus were matched for calories offered, sugar, fat, fiber, and macronutrients, and participants could eat as much as they wanted. On the ultra-processed diet they ate about 500 more calories per day, ate faster, and gained about 0.9 kg. On the unprocessed diet they lost about 0.9 kg <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-21">21</a>]</sup>.</p>
<h3>Volume on the plate</h3>
<p>In a study at Penn State, eating a large, low-calorie salad (300 g, 100 calories) before lunch reduced total calories eaten at the meal by 12%, even counting the salad. The same size salad made calorie-dense with cheese and dressing increased total intake <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-22">22</a>]</sup>.</p>
<h3>Some foods are simply more filling</h3>
<p>Researchers fed people 240-calorie portions of 38 common foods and measured fullness over the next two hours. Compared with white bread, boiled potatoes were rated more than three times as filling, the highest score in the study, while a croissant scored less than half as filling. Fish, oatmeal, apples, and oranges also ranked high <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-23">23</a>]</sup>.</p>
<h3>Protein and fiber</h3>
<p>When protein was raised from 15% to 30% of calories, participants spontaneously ate about 441 fewer calories per day and lost about 4.9 kg over 12 weeks without being told to cut calories <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-24">24</a>]</sup>. In the POUNDS Lost trial, fiber intake predicted weight loss independently of calorie and macronutrient intake <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-25">25</a>]</sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><b>What the evidence supports:</b> Meals built on protein, vegetables, fruit, and minimally processed starches let you eat a larger volume of food for the same calories and feel fuller doing it. That makes a calorie deficit easier to sustain.</p></div>
</section>
<section id="s6">
<h2><span class="n">6</span>Digestion and Regularity</h2>
<p>Stool form is a practical window into how quickly food moves through the gut. The Bristol Stool Form Scale grades stools from type 1 (separate hard lumps) to type 7 (entirely liquid). Types 1 and 2 are associated with slow transit, types 6 and 7 with fast transit, and types 3 and 4 with normal transit <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-26">26</a>]</sup>. The tracker that accompanies this guide uses this scale.</p>
<p>Fiber and fluid are the two main levers. A 2022 meta-analysis of randomized trials found that fiber supplementation improved stool frequency and consistency in adults with constipation, with psyllium, doses above 10 g per day, and use for at least four weeks producing the clearest benefits <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-27">27</a>]</sup>. In the whole-grain trial described in Section 4, the whole-grain diet increased stool weight by about 76 g per day <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-18">18</a>]</sup>.</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:34%"/><col style="width:52%"/><col style="width:14%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Reference value</th><th class="">Men 19-50</th><th class="">Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Adequate fiber intake</td><td class="">38 g per day</td><td class=""><sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-28">28</a>]</sup></td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Adequate total water intake</td><td class="">3.7 L per day (about 125 oz), of which about 3.0 L (about 100 oz) typically comes from beverages</td><td class=""><sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-29">29</a>]</sup></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</section>
<section id="s7">
<h2><span class="n">7</span>What Exercise Does, and What It Does Not Do</h2>
<h3>Exercise alone is a slow way to lose weight</h3>
<p>The American College of Sports Medicine reviewed the evidence and concluded that less than 150 minutes per week of physical activity produces minimal weight loss, more than 150 minutes produces modest loss of about 2 to 3 kg, and 225 to 420 minutes produces about 5 to 7.5 kg. Resistance training alone did not produce meaningful weight loss but increased fat-free mass <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-30">30</a>]</sup>. Combined with the energy compensation described in Section 3, this is why diet does most of the work in fat loss.</p>
<h3>Exercise decides what kind of weight you lose</h3>
<p>During a calorie deficit, resistance training combined with adequate protein is the most effective known way to limit loss of muscle <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-31">31</a>]</sup>. Losing fat while keeping muscle is what changes how a body looks. Losing muscle lowers resting energy expenditure and leaves a person smaller but softer.</p>
<h3>Intervals versus steady effort</h3>
<p>A meta-analysis comparing high-intensity interval training (HIIT) with moderate-intensity continuous training (MICT) in adults with overweight or obesity found similar reductions in body fat, with HIIT taking about 40% less time <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-32">32</a>]</sup>. A 2023 meta-analysis in young and middle-aged adults found HIIT modestly better for waist circumference, body fat percentage, and aerobic fitness, while noting that the clinical size of the differences was limited <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-33">33</a>]</sup>.</p>
<h3>How often to train</h3>
<p>For muscle growth, training a muscle group at least twice per week produces better results than once per week <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-34">34</a>]</sup>. When total weekly volume (sets and reps) is held equal, however, frequency on its own makes little difference. Higher frequency is mainly a way to fit more total work into a week while keeping each session manageable <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-35">35</a>]</sup>. CrossFit's own training guidance suggests that most people can train hard for about three consecutive days before needing rest, recommends a five-day week (three days on, one off, two on, one off) for established athletes, and advises that not every session be performed at maximum effort <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-36">36</a>]</sup>.</p>
</section>
<section id="s8">
<h2><span class="n">8</span>Protein: The Most Useful Nutrient in a Diet</h2>
<h3>It costs more to digest</h3>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><caption>Thermic effect of food by macronutrient <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-4">4</a>]</sup></caption><colgroup><col style="width:28%"/><col style="width:40%"/><col style="width:32%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Macronutrient</th><th class="">Share of its calories used in digestion</th><th class="">Net calories kept from 100 eaten</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Protein</td><td class="">20 to 30%</td><td class="">70 to 80</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Carbohydrate</td><td class="">5 to 10%</td><td class="">90 to 95</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Fat</td><td class="">0 to 3%</td><td class="">97 to 100</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Applying these ranges to a 2,100-calorie diet: raising protein from 15% to 30% of calories moves about 315 calories from carbohydrate and fat into protein, which increases the energy spent on digestion by roughly 50 to 80 calories per day. The larger benefits of protein are the ones below.</p>
<h3>It protects muscle during a deficit</h3>
<p>In a 21-day trial with a 40% calorie deficit, adults eating 1.6 g of protein per kg of body weight per day (twice the recommended dietary allowance) kept significantly more fat-free mass than those eating the allowance of 0.8 g/kg. A third group at 2.4 g/kg did no better than the 1.6 g/kg group <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-37">37</a>]</sup>.</p>
<p>In a four-week trial at McMaster University, young men ate 40% below their energy needs while doing resistance training and high-intensity intervals six days a week. The group eating 2.4 g/kg of protein gained 1.2 kg of lean mass and lost 4.8 kg of fat. The group eating 1.2 g/kg maintained lean mass (a gain of 0.1 kg) and lost 3.5 kg of fat <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-38">38</a>]</sup>. Both groups kept their muscle; the higher protein group changed body composition more.</p>
<p>A meta-analysis of 49 trials found that gains from resistance training level off at about 1.6 g/kg per day, with the upper bound of the estimate near 2.2 g/kg <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-39">39</a>]</sup>. A 2024 meta-analysis of 47 trials in adults losing weight found that intakes above 1.3 g/kg per day were associated with maintained or increased muscle mass, while intakes below 1.0 g/kg were associated with a higher risk of losing it <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-40">40</a>]</sup>. For lean, trained people in a deficit, a widely cited review recommends 2.3 to 3.1 g per kg of lean body mass <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-41">41</a>]</sup>.</p>
<h3>It works best spread out</h3>
<p>A review of per-meal protein research recommends about 0.4 g/kg per meal across at least four meals to reach 1.6 g/kg per day, and up to 0.55 g/kg per meal to reach 2.2 g/kg <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-42">42</a>]</sup>. For an 84 kg (186 lb) person, that is about 34 to 46 g of protein per meal.</p>
</section>
<section id="s9">
<h2><span class="n">9</span>Sleep: The Multiplier</h2>
<p>The most striking sleep study for anyone trying to lose fat put ten adults with overweight on the same moderate calorie deficit for two weeks, twice: once with 8.5 hours in bed per night and once with 5.5 hours. Weight loss was similar. What was lost was not. With short sleep, fat loss fell from 1.4 kg to 0.6 kg (55% less), and loss of fat-free mass rose from 1.5 kg to 2.4 kg (60% more). Participants were also hungrier <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-43">43</a>]</sup>.</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:22%"/><col style="width:36%"/><col style="width:42%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Study</th><th class="">What was tested</th><th class="">Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Spiegel 2004 <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-44">44</a>]</sup></td><td class="">Two nights of 4 hours in bed, young healthy men</td><td class="">Leptin fell 18%, ghrelin rose 28%, hunger rose 24%</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Al Khatib 2017 <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-45">45</a>]</sup></td><td class="">Meta-analysis of partial sleep deprivation trials</td><td class="">About 385 extra calories eaten per day, no meaningful change in calories burned</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Covassin 2022 <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-46">46</a>]</sup></td><td class="">Two weeks at 4 hours per night, free access to food</td><td class="">More than 300 extra calories eaten per day; visceral (deep abdominal) fat rose 11% despite only about 1 lb of weight gain</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Drake 2013 <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-47">47</a>]</sup></td><td class="">400 mg caffeine at 0, 3, or 6 hours before bed</td><td class="">Even 6 hours before bed, caffeine cut total sleep by more than 1 hour</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Sleep also times hormone release. In healthy adults the largest pulse of growth hormone each day occurs shortly after falling asleep, during the first period of deep, slow-wave sleep <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-48">48</a>]</sup>. The American Academy of Sleep Medicine and the Sleep Research Society recommend that adults sleep 7 or more hours per night on a regular basis <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-49">49</a>]</sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><b>What the evidence supports:</b> Short sleep increases appetite, adds hundreds of calories of intake per day, and shifts weight loss away from fat and toward muscle. No training or nutrition strategy in this guide compensates for it.</p></div>
</section>
<section id="s10">
<h2><span class="n">10</span>Measuring What Matters</h2>
<h3>People underestimate what they eat</h3>
<p>In a study published in the <i>New England Journal of Medicine</i>, people who described themselves as unable to lose weight despite eating little had their intake and expenditure measured directly. On average they underreported their food intake by 47% and overreported their physical activity by 51% <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-50">50</a>]</sup>. Estimating by eye is not a reliable way to know what you eat.</p>
<h3>Tracking works</h3>
<p>A systematic review found a consistent association between self-monitoring of diet and weight loss <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-51">51</a>]</sup>. A separate review found that frequent self-weighing was associated with greater weight loss and less regain <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-52">52</a>]</sup>.</p>
<h3>Watches estimate calories poorly</h3>
<p>Stanford researchers tested seven wrist-worn devices against laboratory measurement. None estimated energy expenditure within 20% error. The Apple Watch had the lowest error of the group, but was still well short of laboratory accuracy <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-53">53</a>]</sup>. A watch reading is a useful starting estimate. The trend in body weight over several weeks is the better judge.</p>
<h3>Why the scale jumps around</h3>
<p>Day-to-day body weight moves with water, not just tissue. Each gram of carbohydrate stored in muscle as glycogen is held with roughly three grams of water <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-54">54</a>]</sup>, so a higher-carbohydrate day raises the scale the next morning. Creatine supplementation draws water into muscle cells and increases body mass, mainly in the first weeks of use <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-55">55</a>]</sup>. Sodium intake and the contents of the gut add more noise. A seven-day average smooths most of this out.</p>
<h3>How fast to lose</h3>
<p>Elite athletes randomly assigned to lose weight at 0.7% of body weight per week gained lean mass and improved in strength, while those losing 1.4% per week did not gain lean mass <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-56">56</a>]</sup>. A widely cited review recommends 0.5 to 1% of body weight per week for people trying to keep muscle while leaning out <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-41">41</a>]</sup>.</p>
</section><section class="part" id="part-two">
<p class="part-kicker">Part Two</p>
<h1 class="part-title">Your Plan</h1>
<p class="lede">This half applies Part One to one person: 27 years old, 5 feet 9 inches, 186 lb, training CrossFit in the evenings, eating mostly family-cooked meals with chicken as the main protein. It runs 20 weeks, from Monday, October 5, 2026 to Sunday, February 21, 2027, with a prep week before it. Section references such as "Section 9" point back to the evidence in Part One.</p>
</section>
<section id="s11">
<h2><span class="n">11</span>Your Starting Point</h2>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:30%"/><col style="width:30%"/><col style="width:40%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Measure</th><th class="">Value</th><th class="">Source or method</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Age, height, weight</td><td class="">27 years, 5 ft 9 in (175 cm), 186 lb (84.4 kg)</td><td class="">Self-reported</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Body mass index</td><td class="">27.5</td><td class="">703 × 186 lb ÷ (69 in)<sup>2</sup></td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Total burn on a sedentary day</td><td class="">About 2,100 cal</td><td class="">Apple Watch, normal day without extra movement</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Cross-check of that number</td><td class="">About 2,170 cal</td><td class="">Mifflin-St Jeor resting estimate of 1,810 cal × 1.2 sedentary factor <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-57">57</a>]</sup></td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Total burn on a CrossFit day</td><td class="">About 2,550 cal</td><td class="">2,100 + about 450 for a moderate session (your estimate: 400 to 500)</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Weekly average burn</td><td class="">About 2,420 cal per day</td><td class="">See equation below</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The watch estimate and the equation estimate agree within about 3%, which is reassuring. Both are still estimates, and wrist devices can be off by 20% or more (Section 10). Treat these numbers as a starting point. The first three weeks of morning weigh-ins will show whether they are right, and the Sunday check-in in Section 22 corrects them.</p>
<div class="eq"><div class="eq-line"><i>TDEE</i><sub>avg</sub> = (5 × 2,550 + 2 × 2,100) ÷ 7 = 16,950 ÷ 7 ≈ 2,420 cal/day</div><div class="eq-line"><i>Deficit</i><sub>week</sub> = 5 × (2,550 − 2,100) + 2 × (2,100 − 1,800) = 2,250 + 600 = 2,850 cal</div><div class="eq-line"><i>Expected loss</i> ≈ 2,850 ÷ 3,500 ≈ 0.8 lb/week</div><div class="eq-note">0.8 lb per week is about 0.44% of body weight, at the conservative end of the range associated with keeping muscle (Section 10). The Sunday check-in can speed it up if your weight trend allows.</div></div>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:55%"/><col style="width:45%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Target</th><th class="">Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Training-day calories (Weeks 1 to 12)</td><td class="">2,100</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Rest-day calories (Weeks 1 to 12)</td><td class="">1,800</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Protein, every day</td><td class="">150 to 180 g (1.8 to 2.1 g per kg); never below 140 g</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Projected result at Week 20</td><td class="">About 173 to 177 lb (Section 31)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</section>
<section id="s12">
<h2><span class="n">12</span>The Six Rules, in Priority Order</h2>
<p>When a day goes sideways, protect these in order. A day with good sleep and 160 g of protein that ends 300 calories over target is a decent day. A day exactly on calories with five hours of sleep and 90 g of protein is not.</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl rules"><colgroup><col style="width:5%"/><col style="width:15%"/><col style="width:62%"/><col style="width:18%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class=""></th><th class="">Rule</th><th class="">Daily standard</th><th class="">Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">1</td><td class=""><b>Sleep</b></td><td class="">At least 7.5 hours in bed. Fixed wake time, lights out within 30 minutes of the same time every night, weekends included.</td><td class="">Section 9</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">2</td><td class=""><b>Protein</b></td><td class="">150 to 180 g. Never below 140 g.</td><td class="">Section 8</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">3</td><td class=""><b>Calories</b></td><td class="">Within 100 calories of the day's target on at least 6 of 7 days.</td><td class="">Sections 1, 3</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">4</td><td class=""><b>Training</b></td><td class="">The scheduled session at the assigned RPE. No extra hard days.</td><td class="">Section 7</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">5</td><td class=""><b>Movement</b></td><td class="">At least 7,000 steps. A 30 to 45 minute walk on every rest day.</td><td class="">Section 2</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">6</td><td class=""><b>Tracking</b></td><td class="">Weigh in every morning, log everything you eat, and complete the Sunday check-in.</td><td class="">Section 10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</section>
<section id="s13">
<h2><span class="n">13</span>Why Five Moderate Sessions Instead of Two Hard Ones</h2>
<p>You described two sessions a week at about 1,000 calories each. You were not wiped out afterward, but recovery between sessions took a long time, and you could not have trained that way every day. That long recovery is the constraint that limits how often you can train. Moderate sessions remove it.</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:36%"/><col style="width:24%"/><col style="width:40%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class=""></th><th class="">Before</th><th class="">This plan</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Sessions per week</td><td class="">2</td><td class="">5</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Typical effort</td><td class="">Near maximal</td><td class="">Mostly RPE 7, with 1 to 3 hard days depending on phase</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Calories per session (your estimates)</td><td class="">About 1,000</td><td class="">About 400 to 500 moderate, more on hard days</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Session calories per week</td><td class="">About 2,000</td><td class="">About 2,250 to 2,700</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Resistance-training exposures per week</td><td class="">2</td><td class="">5</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Recovery needed between sessions</td><td class="">Long</td><td class="">Designed to recover within 24 hours</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><b>Why this works better for fat loss.</b> The diet creates most of the fat loss (Section 7). The training job during a deficit is to protect muscle, and that depends on a steady, repeated resistance-training signal plus protein (Sections 7 and 8). Five sessions let you do more total weekly work without any single session being so hard that it needs days of recovery, which is the main benefit of higher frequency in the research (Section 7).</p>
<p><b>What it will not do.</b> The extra sessions will not add calories burned one for one. On average about 28% of activity calories are offset by the body (Section 3), which is why this plan does not count on training to create the deficit. Five sessions is also a real time commitment. If a week gets crowded, the priority order in Section 12 applies: sleep and protein come before a fifth session.</p>
</section>
<section id="s14">
<h2><span class="n">14</span>RPE: How Hard Is "Moderate"?</h2>
<p>RPE stands for <b>Rating of Perceived Exertion</b>, a 1 to 10 scale for how hard an effort feels, adapted from the Borg scale used in exercise science <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-58">58</a>]</sup>. For lifting, each RPE level maps to how many more reps you could have done with good form, called reps in reserve <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-59">59</a>]</sup>. This plan uses two levels: RPE 7 for moderate days and RPE 8 to 9 for hard days.</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl rpe"><colgroup><col style="width:8%"/><col style="width:20%"/><col style="width:17%"/><col style="width:20%"/><col style="width:35%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">RPE</th><th class="">How it feels</th><th class="">Talking</th><th class="">Lifting: reps left in reserve</th><th class="">In a CrossFit workout</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">5</td><td class="">Easy</td><td class="">Full sentences</td><td class="">5 or more</td><td class="">Warm-up pace</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">6</td><td class="">Comfortable, working</td><td class="">Full sentences</td><td class="">4</td><td class="">Could go noticeably faster</td></tr><tr class="hl"><td class=""><b>7</b></td><td class=""><b>Working hard, controlled</b></td><td class="">Short sentences</td><td class=""><b>3</b></td><td class=""><b>A pace you could hold the whole workout with only planned breaks; form stays crisp</b></td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">8</td><td class="">Hard</td><td class="">A few words</td><td class="">2</td><td class="">Pushing; an occasional short unplanned break</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">9</td><td class="">Very hard</td><td class="">Single words</td><td class="">1</td><td class="">Racing the clock; very little left at the end</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">10</td><td class="">Maximal</td><td class="">None</td><td class="">0</td><td class="">All-out; nothing left</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>How to run each type of day in a class</h3>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:20%"/><col style="width:40%"/><col style="width:40%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Part of class</th><th class="">RPE 7 day (moderate)</th><th class="">RPE 8 to 9 day (hard)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Warm-up</td><td class="">As programmed</td><td class="">As programmed</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Strength</td><td class="">Build to a top set you could have done 3 more reps with</td><td class="">Build to a top set with 1 to 2 reps left; never to failure or form breakdown</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Workout loads</td><td class="">Scale so every set is unbroken or broken only as planned</td><td class="">Prescribed (Rx) loads or your heaviest scaled option</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Pace</td><td class="">Breathing hard, but able to say a short sentence</td><td class="">A few words at most</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Finish</td><td class="">You could have gone about 10 to 15% faster</td><td class="">Little or nothing left</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Joint pain</td><td class="">Stop that movement and substitute</td><td class="">Stop that movement and substitute</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout warn"><p><b>Knee rule:</b> muscle burn is fine; pain in the knee joint is not. With the history of a lateral meniscus tear, avoid movements that produce knee pain, especially deep loaded knee bends and twisting on a planted foot, and ask your coach for substitutions. Tell your coach at the start of Week 1 that most of your sessions will be run at RPE 7. This guide is not medical advice; see a sports medicine physician or physical therapist for any new or worsening knee symptoms.</p></div>
</section>
<section id="s15">
<h2><span class="n">15</span>Your Week and Your Day</h2>
<p>The week has a fixed shape for all 20 weeks. Only the RPE assignments and calorie numbers change by phase, and each phase page (Sections 24 to 29) lists them.</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:16%"/><col style="width:22%"/><col style="width:18%"/><col style="width:44%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Day</th><th class="">Session</th><th class="">Day type</th><th class="">Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Monday</td><td class="">CrossFit, 7:30 PM</td><td class="">Training day</td><td class=""></td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Tuesday</td><td class="">CrossFit, 7:30 PM</td><td class="">Training day</td><td class="">First hard day from Phase 2</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Wednesday</td><td class="">Rest</td><td class="">Rest day</td><td class="">30 to 45 minute walk</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Thursday</td><td class="">CrossFit, 7:30 PM</td><td class="">Training day</td><td class="">Hard in Phases 3 to 5 if the recovery check passes</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Friday</td><td class="">CrossFit, 7:30 PM</td><td class="">Training day</td><td class=""></td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Saturday</td><td class="">CrossFit</td><td class="">Training day</td><td class="">Hard day from Week 2; refeed from Phase 2</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Sunday</td><td class="">Rest</td><td class="">Rest day</td><td class="">Walk, measurements when scheduled, Sunday check-in</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><b>Saturday timing.</b> Many boxes run Saturday classes only in the morning. If that is true of yours, you have two options. Take the morning class: drink the electrolytes 45 minutes before, have the shake right after, then eat breakfast, and keep the rest of the day's meals as written. Or move the session to Sunday evening and make Saturday the rest day, with the refeed on Sunday.</p>
<h3>Training-day timeline</h3>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:18%"/><col style="width:20%"/><col style="width:62%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Time</th><th class="">What</th><th class="">Details</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">On waking</td><td class="">Weigh in</td><td class="">After the bathroom, before food or water. Log it.</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">7:00-7:30 AM</td><td class="">Breakfast</td><td class="">About 500 cal, 30 to 38 g protein</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">12:00-1:00 PM</td><td class="">Lunch</td><td class="">About 610 cal, 48 to 50 g protein</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">3:30-4:00 PM</td><td class="">Afternoon snack</td><td class="">About 260 cal, 22 to 25 g protein. Last solid food before training, leaving 3.5 hours to digest.</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">6:45 PM</td><td class="">Electrolytes</td><td class="">Your usual electrolyte mix. Check the label; log any calories it contains.</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">7:30-8:30 PM</td><td class="">CrossFit</td><td class="">At the RPE assigned for the day</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">8:30-9:00 PM</td><td class="">Protein shake</td><td class="">45 g whey and 5 g creatine in 12 to 16 oz water. Nothing else.</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">9:00-9:30 PM</td><td class="">Dinner</td><td class="">About 540 cal, 31 to 39 g protein</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Fixed time</td><td class="">Lights out</td><td class="">8 hours before your wake time. Waking at 6:30 AM means lights out by 10:30 PM.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>Rest-day timeline</h3>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:18%"/><col style="width:20%"/><col style="width:62%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Time</th><th class="">What</th><th class="">Details</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">On waking</td><td class="">Weigh in</td><td class="">Same routine every day.</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">7:00-8:00 AM</td><td class="">Breakfast</td><td class="">About 430 cal, 25 to 34 g protein</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">12:00-1:00 PM</td><td class="">Lunch</td><td class="">About 530 cal, 52 to 53 g protein</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">3:30-4:00 PM</td><td class="">Afternoon shake</td><td class="">45 g whey, 5 g creatine, and an apple</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">6:30-7:30 PM</td><td class="">Dinner</td><td class="">About 550 cal, 36 to 50 g protein</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Evening</td><td class="">Walk</td><td class="">30 to 45 minutes. After dinner works well.</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Fixed time</td><td class="">Lights out</td><td class="">Same time as training days.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</section>
<section id="s16">
<h2><span class="n">16</span>Daily Targets</h2>
<p>Only two numbers are hard targets: <b>calories</b> (within 100 of target) and <b>protein</b> (at least 140 g). Carbohydrate and fat land in the ranges below automatically when you follow the meal plans. They are guardrails to glance at on Sunday, not numbers to chase each day.</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl targets"><colgroup><col style="width:24%"/><col style="width:11%"/><col style="width:11%"/><col style="width:11%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:23%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Day type</th><th class="num">Calories</th><th class="num">Protein (g)</th><th class="num">Carbs (g)</th><th class="num">Fat (g)</th><th class="num">Fiber (g)</th><th class="">Meal plan as written</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Training day</td><td class="num">2,100</td><td class="num">150-180</td><td class="num">210-250</td><td class="num">50-65</td><td class="num">30+</td><td class="">2,101 cal, 175 g P</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Rest day</td><td class="num">1,800</td><td class="num">150-170</td><td class="num">150-190</td><td class="num">50-65</td><td class="num">30+</td><td class="">1,806 cal, 163 g P</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Saturday refeed (Phases 2-3)</td><td class="num">2,500</td><td class="num">150-180</td><td class="num">290-330</td><td class="num">50-65</td><td class="num">30+</td><td class="">2,491 cal, 183 g P</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Targets for Weeks 13 to 20 differ and are listed on each phase page. Every day also includes about 100 oz of water and other drinks (Section 6), 7,000 or more steps, 5 g of creatine, and your multivitamin.</p>
</section>
<section id="s17">
<h2><span class="n">17</span>Weighing and Logging: What, Why, and How</h2>
<h3>What is the point of weighing food?</h3>
<p>Yes, it is mostly for calories and protein, and those are the two numbers that decide the result. Weighing does three specific jobs:</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:28%"/><col style="width:72%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Job</th><th class="">Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Makes the calorie number real</td><td class="">People estimating by eye underreport intake substantially, 47% in one well-known study (Section 10). Calorie-dense foods hide the biggest errors: a heaping spoon of peanut butter is often 30 g (about 175 cal), not the 16 g (94 cal) on the label.</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Makes the Sunday check-in work</td><td class="">The check-in compares what you ate with how your weight moved. If intake is accurate and weight stalls, the target is wrong and gets adjusted. If intake is a guess, you cannot tell a wrong target from a logging problem, and every adjustment is a coin flip.</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Guarantees the protein floor</td><td class="">140 g is hard to reach by accident. Weighing the protein portion at each meal is how you know you got there.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>How to weigh a family-cooked meal</h3>
<p>This takes about a minute once it is routine.</p>
<ol class="steps"><li>Put your empty plate on the scale and press tare so it reads zero.</li><li>Add the chicken or other protein. Note the grams. Press tare.</li><li>Add the rice, roti, or potatoes. Note the grams. Press tare.</li><li>Add the vegetables. Note the grams.</li><li>Log each item using a <b>cooked</b> entry in your app, such as "chicken breast, cooked, roasted" or "white rice, cooked." Raw and cooked entries differ by 2 to 3 times for rice and by about a third for chicken.</li><li>Add an oil entry using the rule below.</li></ol>
<h3>Estimating oil you did not add yourself</h3>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:36%"/><col style="width:64%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Situation</th><th class="">What to log</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Best method</td><td class="">Ask how much oil, butter, or ghee went into the pot. Divide by the number of servings and log your share. One tablespoon is about 14 g and 120 calories.</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Vegetable dish or curry, not visibly oily</td><td class="">5 g oil (1 tsp) per 200 g of the dish</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Visibly oily, glossy, or pooled oil on the plate</td><td class="">10 g oil (2 tsp) per 200 g of the dish</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Plain grilled or roasted chicken</td><td class="">5 g oil per 150 g of chicken unless you know it was cooked dry</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Restaurant meal</td><td class="">Log the closest match, then add 20% to the calories as a buffer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>What to weigh, and for how long</h3>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl defs"><colgroup><col style="width:30%"/><col style="width:50%"/><col style="width:20%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Period</th><th class="">Weigh</th><th class="">Eyeball</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Prep week and Weeks 1 to 3</td><td class="">Everything</td><td class="">Nothing</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Week 4 onward</td><td class="">Protein, starches (rice, roti, potatoes, bread, oats), oils, nut butters, nuts, cheese</td><td class="">Vegetables and whole fruit</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Any time the weight trend stalls for two weeks</td><td class="">Everything, for one full week</td><td class="">Nothing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>Which app</h3>
<p>Use one app and stay with it. <b>Cronometer</b> has a free tier and a database built largely on verified lab sources, which reduces bad entries. <b>MacroFactor</b> is paid and estimates your actual expenditure from your logged intake and weight trend, which automates part of the Sunday check-in. Either works. Set the calorie and protein targets from Section 16, and log as you eat rather than at the end of the day.</p>
</section>
<section id="s18">
<h2><span class="n">18</span>Training-Day Meal Plan (2,100 calories)</h2>
<p>Each meal has two options. Within a meal, the options differ by no more than about 15 calories and are fully interchangeable, so mix and match. All weights are <b>cooked</b> weights except oats (dry) and whey (powder). Food values are from USDA FoodData Central, rounded <sup class="cite">[<a href="#ref-60">60</a>]</sup>.</p>
<h3>Breakfast, 7:00-7:30 AM</h3>
<p class="opt">Option A: Eggs and toast</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl meal"><colgroup><col style="width:34%"/><col style="width:26%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Food</th><th class="">Amount</th><th class="num">Calories</th><th class="num">Protein (g)</th><th class="num">Carbs (g)</th><th class="num">Fat (g)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Eggs, whole</td><td class="">3 large</td><td class="num">216</td><td class="num">19</td><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">14</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Ezekiel 4:9 bread</td><td class="">2 slices</td><td class="num">160</td><td class="num">10</td><td class="num">30</td><td class="num">1</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Berries, fresh or frozen</td><td class="">200 g</td><td class="num">90</td><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">22</td><td class="num">1</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Oil, butter, or ghee</td><td class="">5 g (1 tsp)</td><td class="num">44</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">5</td></tr><tr class="total"><td class=""><b>Meal total</b></td><td class=""></td><td class="num"><b>510</b></td><td class="num"><b>30</b></td><td class="num"><b>53</b></td><td class="num"><b>21</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="opt">Option B: Yogurt bowl</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl meal"><colgroup><col style="width:34%"/><col style="width:26%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Food</th><th class="">Amount</th><th class="num">Calories</th><th class="num">Protein (g)</th><th class="num">Carbs (g)</th><th class="num">Fat (g)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Greek yogurt, plain, nonfat</td><td class="">250 g</td><td class="num">148</td><td class="num">26</td><td class="num">9</td><td class="num">1</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Rolled oats, dry weight</td><td class="">50 g</td><td class="num">190</td><td class="num">7</td><td class="num">34</td><td class="num">3</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Berries, fresh or frozen</td><td class="">150 g</td><td class="num">68</td><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">16</td><td class="num">0</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Peanut butter</td><td class="">16 g (1 tbsp)</td><td class="num">94</td><td class="num">4</td><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">8</td></tr><tr class="total"><td class=""><b>Meal total</b></td><td class=""></td><td class="num"><b>500</b></td><td class="num"><b>38</b></td><td class="num"><b>62</b></td><td class="num"><b>12</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>Lunch, 12:00-1:00 PM</h3>
<p class="opt">Option A: Chicken and rice</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl meal"><colgroup><col style="width:34%"/><col style="width:26%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Food</th><th class="">Amount</th><th class="num">Calories</th><th class="num">Protein (g)</th><th class="num">Carbs (g)</th><th class="num">Fat (g)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Chicken breast, cooked weight</td><td class="">120 g</td><td class="num">198</td><td class="num">37</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">4</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">White rice, cooked weight</td><td class="">180 g</td><td class="num">234</td><td class="num">5</td><td class="num">51</td><td class="num">1</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Vegetables, cooked (non-starchy)</td><td class="">250 g</td><td class="num">88</td><td class="num">6</td><td class="num">18</td><td class="num">1</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Oil, butter, or ghee</td><td class="">10 g (2 tsp)</td><td class="num">88</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">10</td></tr><tr class="total"><td class=""><b>Meal total</b></td><td class=""></td><td class="num"><b>608</b></td><td class="num"><b>48</b></td><td class="num"><b>69</b></td><td class="num"><b>16</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="opt">Option B: Chicken and roti</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl meal"><colgroup><col style="width:34%"/><col style="width:26%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Food</th><th class="">Amount</th><th class="num">Calories</th><th class="num">Protein (g)</th><th class="num">Carbs (g)</th><th class="num">Fat (g)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Chicken breast, cooked weight</td><td class="">120 g</td><td class="num">198</td><td class="num">37</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">4</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Roti, whole wheat</td><td class="">2 medium (about 80 g)</td><td class="num">240</td><td class="num">7</td><td class="num">36</td><td class="num">7</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Vegetables, cooked (non-starchy)</td><td class="">250 g</td><td class="num">88</td><td class="num">6</td><td class="num">18</td><td class="num">1</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Oil, butter, or ghee</td><td class="">10 g (2 tsp)</td><td class="num">88</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">10</td></tr><tr class="total"><td class=""><b>Meal total</b></td><td class=""></td><td class="num"><b>614</b></td><td class="num"><b>50</b></td><td class="num"><b>54</b></td><td class="num"><b>22</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="note">The 10 g of oil is the oil in or on your portion. For family-cooked dishes, use the oil rule in Section 17 instead.</p>
<h3>Afternoon snack, 3:30-4:00 PM</h3>
<p class="opt">Option A: Cottage cheese and fruit</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl meal"><colgroup><col style="width:34%"/><col style="width:26%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Food</th><th class="">Amount</th><th class="num">Calories</th><th class="num">Protein (g)</th><th class="num">Carbs (g)</th><th class="num">Fat (g)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Cottage cheese, 2%</td><td class="">200 g</td><td class="num">168</td><td class="num">22</td><td class="num">9</td><td class="num">5</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Apple</td><td class="">1 medium (about 180 g)</td><td class="num">95</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">25</td><td class="num">0</td></tr><tr class="total"><td class=""><b>Meal total</b></td><td class=""></td><td class="num"><b>263</b></td><td class="num"><b>22</b></td><td class="num"><b>34</b></td><td class="num"><b>5</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="opt">Option B: Yogurt and peanut butter</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl meal"><colgroup><col style="width:34%"/><col style="width:26%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Food</th><th class="">Amount</th><th class="num">Calories</th><th class="num">Protein (g)</th><th class="num">Carbs (g)</th><th class="num">Fat (g)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Greek yogurt, plain, nonfat</td><td class="">200 g</td><td class="num">118</td><td class="num">20</td><td class="num">7</td><td class="num">1</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Berries, fresh or frozen</td><td class="">100 g</td><td class="num">45</td><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">11</td><td class="num">0</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Peanut butter</td><td class="">16 g (1 tbsp)</td><td class="num">94</td><td class="num">4</td><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">8</td></tr><tr class="total"><td class=""><b>Meal total</b></td><td class=""></td><td class="num"><b>257</b></td><td class="num"><b>25</b></td><td class="num"><b>21</b></td><td class="num"><b>9</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>Post-workout shake, 8:30-9:00 PM</h3>
<p class="opt">Protein shake</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl meal"><colgroup><col style="width:34%"/><col style="width:26%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Food</th><th class="">Amount</th><th class="num">Calories</th><th class="num">Protein (g)</th><th class="num">Carbs (g)</th><th class="num">Fat (g)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Whey protein powder</td><td class="">45 g (1.5 scoops)</td><td class="num">180</td><td class="num">36</td><td class="num">4</td><td class="num">2</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Creatine monohydrate</td><td class="">5 g</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Water</td><td class="">12 to 16 oz</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td></tr><tr class="total"><td class=""><b>Meal total</b></td><td class=""></td><td class="num"><b>180</b></td><td class="num"><b>36</b></td><td class="num"><b>4</b></td><td class="num"><b>2</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="note">The plan assumes a whey powder with about 120 calories and 24 g protein per 30 g scoop. If your label differs, scale the grams so the shake gives about 36 g protein.</p>
<h3>Dinner, 9:00-9:30 PM</h3>
<p class="opt">Option A: Chicken, rice, and vegetables</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="tbl meal"><colgroup><col style="width:34%"/><col style="width:26%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/><col style="width:10%"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th class="">Food</th><th class="">Amount</th><th class="num">Calories</th><th class="num">Protein (g)</th><th class="num">Carbs (g)</th><th class="num">Fat (g)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><td class="">Chicken breast, cooked weight</td><td class="">90 g</td><td class="num">148</td><td class="num">28</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">3</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">White rice, cooked weight</td><td class="">200 g</td><td class="num">260</td><td class="num">5</td><td class="num">56</td><td class="num">1</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Vegetables, cooked (non-starchy)</td><td class="">250 g</td><td class="num">88</td><td class="num">6</td><td class="num">18</td><td class="num">1</td></tr><tr class=""><td class="">Oil, butter, or ghee</td><td class="">5 g (1 tsp)</td><td class="num">44</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">5</td></tr><tr class="total"><td class=""><b>Meal total</b></td><td class=""></td><td class="num"><b>540</b></td><td class="num"><b>39</b></td><td class="num"><b>74</b></td><td class="num"><b>10</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
