<p><b>Saturday refeed:</b> eat the training-day plan with lunch rice raised to 330 g and dinner rice raised to 275 g. That adds about 394 calories, nearly all carbohydrate, for a day total of 2,394. Protein and fat stay the same. The research on single refeed days is modest (Section 3); the main purposes here are fuel for the hardest session of the week and a planned break in hunger. It is not a free day: weigh and log as usual. Swaps: 150 g cooked rice can be replaced by 2 roti or 200 g potato (Section 20).</p>
<p><b>Saturday refeed, Week 18 only:</b> eat the training-day plan with lunch rice raised to 330 g and dinner rice raised to 275 g. That adds about 394 calories, nearly all carbohydrate, for a day total of 2,394. Protein and fat stay the same. The research on single refeed days is modest (Section 3); the main purposes here are fuel for the hardest session of the week and a planned break in hunger. It is not a free day: weigh and log as usual. Swaps: 150 g cooked rice can be replaced by 2 roti or 200 g potato (Section 20).</p>
<h3>What this phase does</h3>
<p>Week 18 is the last full deficit week and the retest week. Run your three baseline benchmarks on Tuesday, Thursday, and Saturday, with the same setup as in the prep week. Weeks 19 and 20 cut training intensity and raise calories in two steps so the transition to maintenance is controlled rather than sudden. Take final measurements and photos on Sunday, February 21.</p>
<h3>Checklist</h3>
<ul class="check"><li><span class="box"></span>Week 18: retest all three baseline benchmarks</li><li><span class="box"></span>Weeks 19 and 20: only Saturday is hard</li><li><span class="box"></span>Week 20 Sunday: final measurements and photos</li><li><span class="box"></span>Fill in the final review page of the tracker</li><li><span class="box"></span>Sleep: 7.5 hours in bed, fixed wake time</li><li><span class="box"></span>Steps: 7,000 or more daily; 30 to 45 minute walk on rest days</li><li><span class="box"></span>Creatine 5 g and multivitamin daily</li></ul>
</section>
<section id="s30">
<h2><span class="n">30</span>After Week 20</h2>
<p>Keep adding 100 calories per day each week, using the same dinner-starch change, until your seven-day average holds within about 0.5 lb for two weeks in a row. The intake at that point is your new maintenance. Based on the numbers in this plan it will likely be somewhere around 2,300 to 2,500 calories per day on average, but your scale data decides. Keep protein at 140 g or more, keep training four to five days per week, and keep the morning weigh-in and Sunday check-in. If you want to lose more fat after that, spend at least two to four weeks at maintenance first, then start a new deficit from updated numbers.</p>
</section>
<section id="s31">
<h2><span class="n">31</span>Calendar and Projected Progress</h2>
<p>Projections use the targets in this plan, an expenditure that falls by 7 calories per day for each pound lost, and 3,500 calories per pound. They are a reference line, not a promise: real results will wander around them, and the Sunday check-in keeps you close. "On track" means a seven-day average at or below the projection plus 1.5 lb.</p>
